          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "According to the National Diabetes Association's standards of care, metformin, combined with structured lifestyle changes, is generally the initial pharmacologic treatment for type 2 diabetes when there are no other compelling health conditions. This is due to its long history of use, affordability, neutral effect on weight, and low risk of causing low blood sugar.\n\nHowever, current guidance emphasizes that the choice of first-line therapy can depend on other existing conditions a person may have. This means the decision is not solely based on HbA1c levels in many cases.\n\n(Source: National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary) - \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
